/// known-gap: interp has no overlay classes (`<^>`); the interpreter is being retired
/// `<^>Mood |` ... `xx` declares an overlay class; `overlay calm <^> Mood`
/// makes calm a member. A member gets the class's lines it doesn't write
/// itself (here `hosts:`, `lasts:` and the `on start` block). The class's
/// `dup:` says what a member does on a host that already has another
/// member: with `replace`, angry takes calm's place. A class with no `dup:`
/// lets its members stay together, and bare overlays sit beside any class.
<>Creature |
    focus: 0
    attack: 0
    hp: 10
xx
<^>Mood |
    hosts: Creature
    lasts: 3
    dup: replace
    on start => say("mood on")
xx
overlay calm <^> Mood
    modify
        @focus + 2
    xx
xx
overlay angry <^> Mood
    lasts: 1
    modify
        @attack + 3
    xx
xx
<^>Ailment |
    hosts: Creature
xx
overlay sick <^> Ailment
    modify
        @hp - 2
    xx
xx
overlay delirious <^> Ailment
    modify
        @focus - 1
    xx
xx
overlay blessed
xx
c <> Creature | hp: 10
:overlay!(c, calm)
say("{:overlays_of(c)} focus {c >> focus} attack {c >> attack}")
:overlay!(c, angry)
say("{:overlays_of(c)} focus {c >> focus} attack {c >> attack}")
:overlay!(c, sick)
:overlay!(c, delirious)
:overlay!(c, blessed)
say("{:overlays_of(c)} hp {c >> hp} focus {c >> focus}")
tick()
say("{:overlays_of(c)}")
